  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 220 AM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nTuesday, we are forecast to be under a weak ridge with a digging \ntrough west of the Rocky Mountains. Temperatures are expected to be \npleasant with highs forecast in the 60s for the county warning area \n(CWA). \n\nOur region is forecast to be on the back end of a weak jet streak, \nso we have the potential for breezy conditions Tuesday afternoon for \nthe western CWA. Wind gusts up to 30 mph are possible, although \nlingering cloud cover from earlier precipitation could prevent \nstronger winds from mixing down to the surface. If skies completely \nclear or clear earlier, we could see stronger winds. Models are a \nbit conflicted on the wind potential due to placement of the jet \nstreak and potential cloud cover. NBM probabilities have around a \n40% chance of wind gusts over 35 mph for our Colorado counties. LREF \nhas probabilities of wind gusts over 30 mph less than 10%. \n\nConditions are forecast to remain pleasant on Wednesday with partly \ncloudy skies and high temperatures forecast in the 60s across the \narea. As the day progresses, we transition to more of a southwest \nupper-level flow with a developing trough off the west coast. \n\nThursday and Friday, the forecast remains consistent with high \ntemperatures forecast in the upper 50s to low 60s. Our southwesterly \nupper-level flow will bring chances for rain showers late Thursday \nthrough Friday evening as embedded shortwaves are ejected across the \nregion. Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) are around 20-30% with \nlow QPF amounts. \n\nLooking past Friday, we have the potential to return to more of an \nactive weather pattern based on the forecast track and evolution of \nthe aforementioned trough off the west coast.",
